   "system": "You summarize documents for a town newsletter. Be accurate and brief.",
   "user": "Summarize the following history in three bullet points.\n\nThe weather station on Marrow Hill was built in 1911 by the town's volunteer fire brigade, who wanted an early warning of the autumn gales that flattened their hay ricks. It began as a single wooden hut with a mercury barometer, a wet-and-dry thermometer pair and a rain gauge made from a copper funnel and a graduated glass jar. Every morning at eight o'clock a member of the brigade walked up the hill, read the instruments, and chalked the numbers on a slate board outside the post office. In 1934 the hut was replaced by a stone building with a slate roof, a small stove, and a desk where the readings were copied into bound ledgers. The ledgers survive: forty-one volumes, each covering about two years, with entries in at least nineteen different hands. A wind vane and a cup anemometer were added in 1952, after a storm tore the roof off the school and nobody could agree afterwards how strong the wind had been. The anemometer turned a small dynamo, and a needle on a dial inside the station showed the wind speed in miles per hour. For a while the brigade also kept a sunshine recorder, a glass sphere that burned a line into a card whenever the sun was out, but the cards were hard to buy and the practice stopped in 1967. Automatic instruments arrived in 1998. A solar panel now powers a data logger that records temperature, humidity, pressure, rainfall and wind every ten minutes and sends the readings to the regional office by radio. The old instruments were not thrown away. The barometer hangs in the town hall, the copper rain gauge sits on a shelf in the library, and the slate board is still outside the post office, where children write the day's temperature on it in chalk. Volunteers from the local school still climb the hill once a week to check the solar panel, clear leaves from the rain gauge, and compare the logger's numbers against a hand-held thermometer. When the readings disagree by more than h